0 @I06@ INDI
1 NAME Maria /Branwell/
2 GIVN Maria
2 SURN Branwell
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 APR 1783
2 PLAC Penzance, Cornwall
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 SEP 1821
2 PLAC Haworth, Yorkshire
1 FAMC @F04@
1 FAMS @F01@
1 NOTE A Cornish merchant's daughter from Penzance, well read and, by her own letters, sharp and funny. She travelled north in 1812 to help at her uncle's school near Leeds, met Patrick within weeks, and
2 CONC  married him that December.
2 CONT
2 CONT Six children followed in seven years. She died at Haworth in September 1821, most likely of cancer, when her youngest was barely a year old.
0 @I07@ INDI
1 NAME Elizabeth /Branwell/
2 GIVN Elizabeth
2 SURN Branwell
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1776
2 PLAC Penzance, Cornwall
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 OCT 1842
2 PLAC Haworth, Yorkshire
1 FAMC @F04@
1 NOTE Maria's elder sister, known in the household simply as *Aunt Branwell*. She came from Penzance to nurse her sister and stayed twenty years, raising six children in a cold parsonage she is said never
2 CONC  to have stopped comparing unfavourably with Cornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her legacy paid for the sisters' first book, the 1846 *Poems by Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell*.
0 @I08@ INDI
1 NAME Maria /Brontë/
2 GIVN Maria
2 SURN Brontë
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 23 APR 1814
2 PLAC Hightown, Yorkshire
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 MAY 1825
2 PLAC Haworth, Yorkshire
1 FAMC @F01@
1 NOTE The eldest child, remembered by Charlotte as precociously grown-up. She was sent with Elizabeth to the Clergy Daughters' School at Cowan Bridge, fell ill there, and was brought home to die in May
2 CONC  1825, aged eleven.
2 CONT
2 CONT The school became Lowood in *Jane Eyre*.
0 @I09@ INDI
1 NAME Elizabeth /Brontë/
2 GIVN Elizabeth
2 SURN Brontë
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 FEB 1815
2 PLAC Hightown, Yorkshire
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 JUN 1825
2 PLAC Haworth, Yorkshire
1 FAMC @F01@
1 NOTE Sent to Cowan Bridge with her sister Maria and brought home in the same summer. She died six weeks after her, aged ten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their deaths ended the school experiment: the four surviving children were taught at home, and began writing there.
0 @I10@ INDI
1 NAME Charlotte /Brontë/
2 GIVN Charlotte
2 SURN Brontë
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 APR 1816
2 PLAC Thornton, Yorkshire
1 DEAT
2 DATE 31 MAR 1855
2 PLAC Haworth, Yorkshire
1 FAMC @F01@
1 FAMS @F02@
1 NOTE Taught at Roe Head, then spent two years in Brussels at the Pensionnat Heger, which she reworked twice into fiction. In 1846 she found Emily's poems by accident and pushed all three sisters into print
2 CONC  under the names Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell; the volume sold two copies.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Jane Eyre* followed in 1847 and made her famous while nobody knew who she was.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Novels
2 CONT
2 CONT - *Jane Eyre* (1847)
2 CONT - *Shirley* (1849)
2 CONT - *Villette* (1853)
2 CONT - *The Professor* (written first, published 1857)
2 CONT
2 CONT > Reader, I married him.
2 CONT
2 CONT She did, in June 1854 — her father's curate, after refusing him once and her father refusing him twice. She died the following March, in the early months of a pregnancy, the last of the six children
2 CONC  to go.
